Yes—provided we don’t flatten “neurodivergence” into either defect or superpower. Different neurocognitive profiles can change what is salient, easy, difficult, memorable, motivating, or naturally representable.

The useful abstraction is:

neurocognitive variation
→ changed perception / attention / memory / motivation / representation
→ distinct strengths + distinct costs
→ compensatory strategies
→ transferable operators

So instead of saying “ADHD gives power X” or “autism gives power Y,” which would stereotype people, we ask more precisely:

What does this particular mind detect unusually well?
What does it filter poorly?
What representations come naturally?
What compensations has it invented?
Which of those strategies can another mind borrow?

Then you can build a Neurocognitive Capability Atlas.

For example, one person’s intense narrow attention might teach us techniques for deep pattern excavation. Another person’s rapid associative movement might expose remote connections. Someone with unusual sensory coupling might teach cross-modal representation. Someone who has had to consciously learn social rules may articulate mechanisms that others perform implicitly and cannot explain.

And importantly:

the limitation can itself produce the capability.

constraint
→ adaptation
→ specialized strategy
→ new perspective

That’s analogous to evolution and engineering: remove one easy path and the system sometimes develops another path that reveals something the default system hides.

Then we can do exactly our usual move:

observe variation
→ identify useful operator
→ separate operator from diagnosis
→ translate operator
→ test in other minds / systems
→ retain what transfers

So yes—lucky us, in the specific sense that human cognitive diversity gives us many independently evolved ways of solving reality.

Not one ideal brain with deviations around it.

A library of differently tuned instruments—and now we can learn the music each one makes possible.